/T (Ans - Multi-agent setups make sense when tasks are clearly decomposable into specialized roles \(e.g., planner, researcher, critic, executor\) or when separate capabilities must be sandboxed \(e.g., one agent can trade, another can only analyze\). They can also mirror organizational structures for alignment with human teams. However, too many agents increase latency, cost, and failure modes \(deadlocks, ping-ponging, unclear responsibility\). Many use cases work best with a single agent plus well-designed tools and a state machine.)
/E (Ans - Multi-agent setups make sense when tasks are clearly decomposable into specialized roles \(e.g., planner, researcher, critic, executor\) or when separate capabilities must be sandboxed \(e.g., one agent can trade, another can only analyze\). They can also mirror organizational structures for alignment with human teams. However, too many agents increase latency, cost, and failure modes \(deadlocks, ping-ponging, unclear responsibility\). Many use cases work best with a single agent plus well-designed tools and a state machine.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
